Note: reading the special characters on this page will require that you have a Tibetan character font, such as [http://www.thdl.org/xml/show.php?xml=/tools/tibfonts.xml&l=uva10928423419921|Tibetan Machine Uni] (free download), installed. Reproducing these characters requires an input tool such as [http://byak.sinp.msu.ru/tise/|TisÃ©] (free download).

a is normally unmarked. In a syllable with no other sounds, it is written à½¨à¼‹ (a). Note that in syllables which have "a" as the vowel, Tibetan's proclivity for stacking consonants is important: those that are stacked occur before the vowel, while those that are not stacked occur after it. This rule was not followed scrupulously in Tibetan, but it should be in Lojban. However, TisÃ© has trouble rendering and/or prefers not to render some clusters of consonants. One can force it to stack consonants by typing + between them. The only instances I know of in which this doesn't work are those in which the second consonant is f or v (which figures, because these letters are not originally Tibetan - they were added to their script to render Chinese words). In this instance, whenever TisÃ© fails to render it correctly, the v must be written separately. If this is done, the a must be written out as à½¨ afterward; otherwise, it will be assumed that the v occurs at the end of the syllable. Note that this is not necessary in the case of other vowels, which are marked (e.g. zov à½Ÿà½¼à½–à¼¹ and zvoà¼‹à½Ÿà½–à¼¹à½¼)
 
b à½– (b)

